                "ImageDescription": {
                  "value": "Timeline diagram comparing a prospective cohort study, a retrospective cohort study and a case-control study, with smoking as the exposure and lung cancer as the outcome. The horizontal axis is years from the start of the study, from −20 to +20, and the dashed vertical line at 0 marks the start of the study. In each row an orange arrow shows the direction of inquiry: it leaves the groups the study starts with and points to what the study then measures. Gray boxes are exposure groups (smokers and nonsmokers). White boxes are outcome groups (lung cancer and no lung cancer).\n<ul><li>Prospective cohort: the study starts at year 0 with smokers and nonsmokers and follows them up for 20 years to see who develops lung cancer. It compares the outcome in the two groups, with the risk ratio or the odds ratio.</li>\n<li>Retrospective cohort: the study starts from records of smokers and nonsmokers made 20 years earlier and follows them up in past records to the start of the study. It compares the outcome in the two groups, with the risk ratio or the odds ratio.</li>\n<li>Case-control: the study starts at year 0 with cases (people with lung cancer) and controls (people without lung cancer) and looks back at their smoking history. It compares exposure, using the odds ratio.</li></ul>\nThe 20-year span is illustrative and does not come from a particular study.",
                  "source": "commons-desc-page"
                },
